From 4b3127d6bad1e2ed07d1cd3c3dd7c781db00bf3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 04:05:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(seo+budget): metadata, sitemap, robots, structured
 data, bundle guard

SEO, all of it content independent
- lib/site.ts: one source for domain, description, email, socials. NOTE:
  SITE_URL currently guesses https://nexelite.in and must be confirmed.
  Metadata, canonicals, OG and the sitemap all resolve against it.
- layout.tsx gains metadataBase (without it every relative OG and canonical
  URL resolves against localhost in production), title template, canonical,
  Open Graph, Twitter card, robots directives and a themeColor viewport.
- app/sitemap.ts and app/robots.ts. The sitemap reads lib/work.ts, so every
  case study added appears without anyone maintaining a list.
- Organization JSON-LD in the document body.

Bundle guard
- scripts/check-bundle.mjs, wired as npm run check:bundle and folded into
  npm run verify alongside tsc, eslint and build.
- Guards the thing that actually matters: that Three.js stays in exactly one
  chunk and never gets duplicated into a shared or entry chunk.

Measured limitation, recorded rather than papered over
- next/dynamic does NOT keep the Three.js chunk out of first load. Turbopack
  emits an eager <script async> for it in the prerendered HTML, so the
  browser downloads 544kb during first load even though the corridor has not
  rendered. Async, so parse and paint are unblocked, but it is real
  bandwidth. An idle-triggered bare import() was tried and behaves the same,
  so it was reverted in favour of the simpler idiomatic form.
  The guard now reports this every build so it stays visible. Options are
  documented in scripts/check-bundle.mjs.

Verified: tsc clean, eslint clean, build passes, 5 routes, budget passes.
---
